import json,re,sys
cells=[]
def md(s): cells.append({"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{},"source":s})
def code(s): cells.append({"cell_type":"code","metadata":{},"execution_count":None,"outputs":[],"source":s.strip('\n')})
def body(fn,start):
    s=open(fn).read(); s=s[s.index(start):]
    s=s.replace("pd.read_parquet('v2/kickstarter_merged_v2.parquet',columns=['id','pledged','static_usd_rate','usd_pledged'])","raw[['id','pledged','static_usd_rate','usd_pledged']]")
    return s.replace("'v2/","out+'/")
def chunks(s,marks):
    idx=[s.index(m) for m in marks]+[len(s)]
    return [s[:idx[0]]]+[s[idx[i]:idx[i+1]] for i in range(len(marks))]
md("# 03 — Models · Crowdfund Compass (Group 11)\nGoal: predict before launch whether a Kickstarter campaign will be funded, compare class models with a baseline, and test the result honestly on future years.\n\nWorkflow: 1) Setup → 2) Import and prepare → 3) Baselines and class models (validation year 2024) → 4) Beyond-class model, all-years vs recent model, final test on 2025 and January–June 2026 → 5) Walk-forward, calibration details, support models → 6) Conclusions.\n\n**Time split, never random:** models are trained on earlier launches and judged on later ones. **Headline metric:** AUC *within category* (computed inside each of the 174 categories, then averaged), because our sample over-represents funded campaigns differently in each category.\n\nRuntime: about 20 minutes in Colab. *AI use: code written with Claude (Fable 5.2), 16.11.2026; reviewed by the team.*")
md("## 1) Setup\n`features.py` and `modeling.py` are our shared modules (the app uses the same files). Put them in the Drive folder `project`.")
code("import sys, time, json, warnings\nwarnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n!pip -q install vaderSentiment lightgbm optuna\nimport numpy as np, pandas as pd, joblib, os\nimport xgboost as xgb, lightgbm as lgb, optuna\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression, LinearRegression\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV, TimeSeriesSplit\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, OneHotEncoder\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\nfrom sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\nfrom sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score, confusion_matrix, brier_score_loss, silhouette_score, roc_curve\noptuna.logging.set_verbosity(optuna.logging.WARNING); pd.set_option('display.width', 250); pd.set_option('display.max_rows', 200)\n\n# from google.colab import drive; drive.mount('/content/drive')\nproject = 'drive/My Drive/crowdfund_compass'      # folder with features.py and modeling.py\npath = 'drive/My Drive/ks_scrapes/merged_v2'      # folder with the merged parquet parts\nout = project + '/models'; os.makedirs(out, exist_ok=True)   # models and result tables are saved here\nsys.path.insert(0, project)\nimport features as F, modeling as M")
md("## 2) Import and prepare\nBuild the features (notebook 02), then `M.prepare` adds label codes and assigns each campaign to a period: `<=2022`, `2023`, `2024` (validation), `2025` and `2026H1` (test). Launches from July 2026 on are dropped: they ended after the last full crawl.")
code("raw = F.load_merged(path)\nX, tables = F.build_features(raw)\nX.to_parquet(out + '/features.parquet', index=False)")
s1=chunks(body('stage1.py',"D=M.prepare"),["# 1 baselines","# 2 class models","# feature-set variants","# category norms on a second"])
md("## 3) Baselines and class models, judged on the validation year 2024\nTraining data here: launches of 2023 (the first year where video and pre-launch page are known for every campaign).\n\n**Text score (class: text mining).** Title and blurb are turned into TF-IDF word weights, and a logistic regression learns which words go with funded campaigns. Each campaign gets one number, `text_score`. Training rows are scored out-of-fold (by a model that never saw them), so the score cannot leak a campaign's own outcome. *Stacking a text model into a second model is beyond class.*")
code(s1[0])
md("### 3.1 Baselines\nAny model must beat two naive rules: always predict \"funded\", and predict the funding rate of the campaign's category.")
code(s1[1])
md("### 3.2 Class models\nLogistic regression, decision tree, random forest and XGBoost. Tree, forest and XGBoost are tuned with `GridSearchCV`; the folds are time-ordered (`TimeSeriesSplit`), so tuning never looks into the future. The depth-3 tree is deliberately small: the app will show its path as a plain explanation.")
code(s1[2])
md("### 3.3 Which feature groups matter?\nThe same XGBoost is retrained without one group at a time, and with only the six features a typical Kaggle Kickstarter dataset offers (category, country, goal, duration, launch month, title length).")
code(s1[3])
md("### 3.4 Category norms on a second split\nThe three category-norm features lowered the within-category AUC above. Before dropping them we confirm it on a second split that does not touch the test years (train 2021–22, evaluate 2023).")
code(s1[4])
md("**Interpretation:** every class model beats both baselines clearly. Logistic regression, random forest and XGBoost are close to each other; the single tree is weaker. Category norms lower the within-category AUC on both splits, so they are dropped. Title capitalisation was dropped later as well (it made the Pitch score depend on Title Case): 32 features remain in the final model (30 without video and pre-launch page).")
s2=chunks(body('stage2.py',"BP=joblib.load"),["# ---------- (a)","# ---------- (b)","# ---------- (c)","# ---------- (d)","# ---------- (e)","# ---------- (f)","# ---------- (g)","# ---------- save"])
md("## 4) Final models and test\nHelper functions: `XG` trains XGBoost with the tuned settings, `logit_pipe` builds the logistic regression pipeline, `with_text` adds the text score without leakage.")
code(s2[0])
md("### 4.1 Beyond class: LightGBM tuned with Optuna\n*Why:* to check whether a more modern boosting library with a smarter search beats the class method. *In plain words:* LightGBM builds many small decision trees like XGBoost, just faster. Optuna tries settings one after another and learns from earlier tries which region to explore next, instead of testing a fixed grid.")
code(s2[1])
md("### 4.2 All-years model (A) against recent-years model (M)\nA uses every year but cannot use video / pre-launch page (unknown for older projects, and \"unknown\" depends on the outcome). M uses only launches from 2023 on, with those two features.")
code(s2[2])
md("### 4.3 Test: 2025 and January–June 2026\nEvery model is retrained on 2023–2024 with the settings chosen above and evaluated once on the test years.\n\n**Goal rule (beyond class).** The final model is the tuned XGBoost with one extra rule: it may only learn that a higher goal lowers the chance of funding, never the opposite (`monotone_constraints`). *Why:* without it the model said in 6% of campaigns that lowering the goal lowers the chance, which makes no sense as advice. *In plain words:* we tell the model one thing we know about the world, and it costs nothing in accuracy.")
code(s2[3])
md("### 4.4 Calibration (beyond class)\n*Why:* the app shows a probability, so 70% must mean about 70 in 100. *In plain words:* Platt scaling is a small correction curve learned on the validation year 2024 that stretches or squeezes the model's scores so that predicted chances match observed frequencies. The Brier score measures the remaining gap (lower is better).")
code(s2[4])
md("### 4.5 Confusion matrix, results by parent category, capped vs other categories")
code(s2[5])
md("### 4.6 Business metric\nA coach can only help part of a cohort. If the coach takes the 30% of campaigns the model rates riskiest, what share of the campaigns that later fail is among them?")
code(s2[6])
md("### 4.7 Level correction to Kickstarter's official rates (beyond class)\n*Why:* our sample holds most funded campaigns but well under half of the failed ones, so sample probabilities are too high. *In plain words:* for each parent category we shift the odds by the gap between the funded rate in our sample and Kickstarter's official all-time rate. The official rate is an all-time average and recent rates are higher, so the app shows a range: corrected value (low end) to sample value (high end).")
code(s2[7])
md("### 4.8 Save the models for the app")
code(s2[8])
s3=chunks(body('stage3.py',"BP=joblib.load"),["# ---- 1)","# ---- 2)","# ---- 3)","# ---- 4)","# ---- 5)"])
