code(s3[0])
md("### 5.1 Walk-forward\nTrain up to year t−1, test on year t, for every year from 2019. Two variants: all earlier years, or only the last two. (30 features: no video / pre-launch page, no text score, so that every year is comparable.)")
code(s3[1])
md("### 5.2 Calibration after Platt scaling, and check of the level correction")
code(s3[2])
md("### 5.3 Support model: typical money raised (class: linear regression)\nLinear regression on log10 of the USD raised. \"Error factor ×1.8\" means the estimate is typically off by a factor of 1.8 in either direction.")
code(s3[3])
md("### 5.4 Campaign archetypes (k-means; clustering is a class topic, its use here is descriptive)\n*In plain words:* k-means sorts campaigns into a few groups that look alike on goal, duration, preparation time, creator experience, video, pre-launch page and text length. The silhouette score (0 to 1) says how clearly the groups are separated.")
code(s3[4])
md("### 5.5 Similar campaigns (nearest neighbours, beyond class)\n*In plain words:* for a new campaign we look up the 10 most similar past campaigns in the same parent category and show how many of them were funded. As a check, that share alone already predicts 2025 outcomes.")
code(s3[5])
md("## 6) Conclusions\n1. All class models beat the baselines by a wide margin; XGBoost with the goal rule is the final model (within-category AUC 0.859 on 2025, 0.866 on January–June 2026; without the rule 0.859 and 0.867).\n2. The model family matters little (logistic regression 0.857, random forest 0.858, LightGBM 0.866). The features matter: 6 Kaggle-style features reach 0.753.\n3. Two recent years of data do as well as all history inside a category.\n4. Probabilities are well calibrated on the sample after Platt scaling, but the sample is too optimistic; the app shows a range down to the level implied by Kickstarter's official rates.\n5. If a coach takes the riskiest 30%, 76% of the later failures in our sample are among them (random: 30%).\n6. Limits: missing failed campaigns still inflate all scores; associations are not causes.")
